Fifty-six of the 61 declarative fixtures under gate/, decide/, sequence-a/ and sequence-c/,
and all seven canonical byte vectors under canonical/, were promoted — copied in id, file
name and content — from the TypeScript reference implementation. The other five were authored
here; the section "Authored here, not promoted" below says which and why. One of the fifty-six
has since been amended in place rather than re-promoted: "Amended here at v0.1.3", below.

  repository  Sakwala/affiant-ts   https://github.com/Sakwala/affiant-ts
  commit      aa485f2946e4678a357544e0a115dc88536cca44
  package     @affiant/core 0.1.0-alpha.0
  path        packages/core/test/fixtures/{gate,decide,sequence-a,sequence-c,canonical}
  runner      packages/core/src/testing.ts, published as @affiant/core/testing
  promoted    2026-09-04

The seven canonical byte vectors were re-promoted at v0.1.1, from `aed3bfb` to `f041cdd`,
and are byte-identical at the commit above. At v0.1.0 every one of them described a SEED-SHAPED record: `operationType`
"WriteUpdate" where v0.1 says `create` or `update`; `allowedValues` and `pattern` on the
fields and `warnings` and `requiresConfirmation` on the record, all four of which v0.1
puts on the card envelope because nothing swears to them; `evidence` where a tag now says
`note`, and no `at` and no `binding` on a tag at all; and no `protocolVersion`, no
`conversationTurn`, no `createdAt`. `schemas/0.1.0/affidavit.schema.json` refuses all
seven. They were promoted in that shape because they came from the reference
implementation before it was aligned to the v0.1 wire, and nothing here checked them
afterwards.

SR-1 defines the canonical form over the accepted state of the Affidavit *as the schema
defines it*, so a vector whose input is not that record pins the bytes of a document this
protocol does not have. The reference implementation's generator now builds each vector's
input through its aligned model, every byte and every digest moved, and
`conformance/lint/lint.mjs` validates every vector's `input` — and its `amendedInput`,
the accepted state an amended vector's bytes are taken over — against the Affidavit
schema on every push. The two vectors that were bare JSON values are now Affidavits
carrying the same key-ordering and number-form cases inside a field's value, so every
vector is a record the schema can be held against.

Two declarative fixtures were re-promoted at v0.1.2, from `f041cdd` to the commit above:
`sequence-a/approve-round-trip` and `decide/amend-recompute`. Each pinned an `expect.canonicalHash`
the reference implementation's **runtime** canonical path produced, and that path disagreed with the
implementation's own document path: its Affidavit model omitted `protocolVersion` and only its wire
writer added it, so the bytes a Docket row hashed were not the bytes of the same record on a card.
SR-1's form is over the Affidavit as `schemas/0.1.0/affidavit.schema.json` defines it, and that
schema requires `protocolVersion` on the record — which is why all seven canonical vectors carry it
and these two hashes did not. The runtime form was corrected and both hashes were regenerated from
it:

  sequence-a/approve-round-trip  776b7b40…6837275  ->  2ce4c4af…840eca9
  decide/amend-recompute         8d1579d7…4ecff6a  ->  d389401d…3bd3402

The corrected bytes are the previous bytes with one key inserted. Nothing else in either document
changed, the seven canonical vectors are untouched, and the other 54 declarative fixtures are
byte-identical at `aed3bfb`, at `f041cdd` and at the commit above.

One document was re-promoted before v0.1.0 was tagged, from `c4591ea` to `aed3bfb`:
`sequence-a/typed-inputs-on-the-card`. Its `expect.card.fields[]` stated `allowedValues` and
`pattern`, which is where the reference implementation carried them before it was aligned to
this version's wire; v0.1 puts both on the card envelope, in `presentation`, so the fixture
named keys a v0.1 card does not have and every driver had to map them. It now states
`expect.card.presentation[]`, and `conformance/RUNNER.md` §4.2 and
`conformance/fixture.schema.json` were changed with it. That was possible only because no
version tag existed yet: a parity manifest cites a fixture by id, so one produced against one
set of bytes says nothing about another. Every run published under conformance/results/ names
on its own record the ref it was produced against, which is how a reader tells which set of
bytes a given claim is about.

Authored here, not promoted (v0.1.3):

  gate/18-inference-presence-computed-from-the-utterance.json
  gate/19-inference-port-literal-unconfirmed.json
  gate/20-inference-port-span-fails-the-boundary.json
  gate/21-inference-case-folds-and-the-digest-is-the-utterances.json
  gate/22-inference-empty-value-is-nothing-reported.json

PV-3 at v0.1.3 states that an implementation establishes presence from the unmodified
utterance itself, and that the inference port's `presence` and `utteranceSpan` are hints it
verifies the same way. Neither implementation did that when the rule was amended — the .NET
step graded `Conversation` only where the port's JSON said `presence: "literal"`, and no
shipped inference port asks a model for `presence` at all — so there was no reference
behaviour to promote. The first four are the amendment's negative oracle instead: all four must
fail against Sakwala/affiant 1.0.0-beta.3, and conformance/ORACLE.md says why each does.

The fifth, gate/22, is not on that list and is not evidence about the release. It pins the
half of the rule that says what a port did NOT report: a value of null, an object, an array or
the empty string is not a value a field can carry, so nothing is merged, the field stays Empty,
and a proposal whose only field is Empty is refused by GT-3. 1.0.0-beta.3 already skips an
empty value text (src/Affiant.Core/Filters/TaskInferenceStep.cs at that tag, the guard on
ReadScalarText) and so passes it; the implementation the fixture is aimed at is the TypeScript
core, which admitted the empty string as Inferred at the port's confidence and would file the
proposal instead of refusing it (packages/core/src/gate/pipeline.ts at 0.1.0-alpha.0). It is
authored here for the same reason as the other four: no implementation had the behaviour to
copy.

Amended here at v0.1.3, not re-promoted:

  sequence-a/04-picker-external-binding.json

This one was promoted with the other fifty-five and its bytes have now moved, so the diff
below reports it. What changed is the `status` field's expectation, and only that: it pinned

  { "name": "status", "value": "Active", "source": "Conversation", "bound": false }

over an utterance that reads "Set invoice INV-2 to Active and give it to the person I picked",
with the scripted port reporting `presence: "literal"` and `utteranceSpan: null`. `bound: false`
was the shipped behaviour of both implementations — a binding only where the port named a span
— and not the rule. Under PV-3 at v0.1.3 the implementation finds "Active" itself, at offset 21
for six UTF-16 code units, so the field is bound to the span it was read from. The expectation
now pins `bound: true`, `bindingKind: "utterance-span"` and the span itself, digest included,
which is also the first place in the suite that states which occurrence was found and that the
digest is taken over the utterance's own bytes.

It was amended rather than re-promoted because the TypeScript reference does not implement the
amended rule yet; there is nothing to re-promote from. When it does, this fixture is expected
to be identical to the reference's own again, and the diff below should fall silent for it.
The amendment also puts this fixture on the beta.3 oracle list (conformance/ORACLE.md): the
release mints no binding where the port named no span, so it now fails, which is what a
negative oracle is for.

A fixture authored here is held to the same bar as a promoted one: it is listed in
MANIFEST.json, validated against conformance/fixture.schema.json by the lint, named on the
rule it checks, and cited back from that rule's *Checked by* line. What it cannot claim is
the thing promotion buys — that a working implementation is green against these exact bytes
— which is why the oracle rows matter more here, not less.

Byte-identical, and deliberately so — the one amendment above aside. A parity
manifest cites a fixture by its id, so a rename silently changes what a
published document refers to; and a fixture edited on the way in would no longer
be the document the reference implementation is green against, which is the only
thing that makes "this implementation passes it and that one does not" a
comparison rather than an opinion.

The format these documents are written in is conformance/RUNNER.md; the schema they are
checked against is conformance/fixture.schema.json; the index that says which rules each
one checks and which of them must fail against a release known to be broken is
conformance/fixtures/MANIFEST.json, section "conformance".

